  <div class="wrap">





<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A distinctly Canadian tax-system mechanism, given dedicated treatment</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3805 governs the RECOGNITION, MEASUREMENT, and PRESENTATION of INVESTMENT tax CREDITS (ITCs) – REDUCTIONS in INCOME taxes OTHERWISE payable, EARNED by INCURRING specified TYPES of expenditures UNDER Canadian FEDERAL and PROVINCIAL tax LEGISLATION. GENUINELY distinctively, THIS is a DEDICATED, standalone SECTION addressing a TAX mechanism SPECIFIC to the CANADIAN tax SYSTEM, MOST commonly ASSOCIATED with the SR&amp;ED PROGRAM (Section 4).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Tax credits tied to specified qualifying expenditures</div><div class="principle">This Section applies to INVESTMENT tax CREDITS – GOVERNED separately FROM the BROADER government ASSISTANCE framework IN Section 3800 (see the COMPANION poster), GIVEN their SPECIFIC, direct INTERACTION with the INCOME tax system.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>What Is an Investment Tax Credit</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A reduction in taxes otherwise payable, earned through qualifying expenditures</div><div class="text-supporting">AN investment TAX credit is a REDUCTION in INCOME taxes OTHERWISE payable, EARNED by an ENTERPRISE incurring SPECIFIED types OF expenditures (e.g. QUALIFYING research AND development COSTS, or CERTAIN capital INVESTMENTS in SPECIFIC regions OR industries) UNDER federal OR provincial TAX legislation – a TAX-system incentive MECHANISM, RATHER than a DIRECT cash GRANT.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The SR&amp;ED Program – the Most Common Source of ITCs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Canada's Scientific Research and Experimental Development incentive</div><div class="text-supporting">THE Scientific RESEARCH and EXPERIMENTAL Development (SR&amp;ED) PROGRAM is the MOST common SOURCE of INVESTMENT tax CREDITS for CANADIAN private ENTERPRISES – PROVIDING tax CREDITS for QUALIFYING research AND development EXPENDITURES, a GENUINELY significant INCENTIVE for TECHNOLOGY, manufacturing, AND other INNOVATION-driven private ENTERPRISES across CANADA.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Recognition Criteria – Reasonable Assurance</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The same reasonable assurance threshold as government assistance generally</div><div class="text-supporting">AN investment TAX credit is RECOGNIZED when THERE is reasonable ASSURANCE that the ENTERPRISE has MET, or WILL meet, the REQUIREMENTS to BE entitled TO the credit – MIRRORING the SAME "reasonable ASSURANCE" recognition THRESHOLD applied MORE broadly under SECTION 3800.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Presentation for ITCs Related to Depreciable Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The same cost-reduction / deferred-credit choice as Section 3800</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE an ITC RELATES to the ACQUISITION of a DEPRECIABLE asset, it MAY be DEDUCTED from the COST of the ASSET (REDUCING future DEPRECIATION), OR recorded AS a DEFERRED credit AMORTIZED to INCOME over the ASSET'S useful LIFE – the SAME genuine PRESENTATION choice AVAILABLE under Section 3800'S own GOVERNMENT assistance FRAMEWORK.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Presentation for ITCs Related to Current Expenditures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Reducing the related expense, or presented as income</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE an ITC RELATES to CURRENT expenditures (MOST commonly, SR&amp;ED-qualifying RESEARCH and DEVELOPMENT costs), it IS generally RECOGNIZED as a REDUCTION of the RELATED expense – THOUGH presenting IT as a SEPARATE item OF income IS also GENERALLY acceptable.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Relationship to Section 3800's Government Assistance Framework</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A specialized companion, not a wholly separate model</div><div class="text-supporting">CONCEPTUALLY, ITCs SHARE the SAME underlying RECOGNITION philosophy AS the BROADER government ASSISTANCE framework IN Section 3800 – BOTH require REASONABLE assurance BEFORE recognition, and BOTH offer SIMILAR presentation CHOICES for ASSET-related versus EXPENSE-related amounts. Section 3805 EXISTS as its OWN dedicated SECTION primarily BECAUSE investment TAX credits INTERACT directly WITH the enterprise'S own INCOME tax CALCULATION (SECTION 3465 – see the COMPANION poster), WARRANTING specific, TAX-focused guidance BEYOND Section 3800'S more GENERAL treatment.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – an SR&amp;ED Credit on Current Expenditures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Reducing R&amp;D expense directly</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A technology company's SR&ED claim</div><div class="wk-body">A private technology company incurs $800,000 of qualifying SR&ED research and development expenditures during the year, earning a $280,000 investment tax credit, with reasonable assurance of entitlement.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY recognizes the $800,000 of R&amp;D EXPENSE, REDUCED by the <strong>$280,000</strong> ITC, FOR a NET R&amp;D expense OF $520,000 – REFLECTING the GENUINELY significant COST offset the SR&amp;ED program PROVIDES.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – an ITC Reducing the Cost of Capital Equipment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Lower future depreciation, mirroring Section 3800's own mechanics</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A regional capital investment credit</div><div class="wk-body">A manufacturing company purchases $2,000,000 of qualifying equipment in a designated region, earning a $150,000 investment tax credit toward the purchase. The equipment has a 10-year useful life, and the company elects the cost reduction approach.</div><div class="wk-answer">The COMPANY records the EQUIPMENT at $2,000,000 − $150,000 = $1,850,000, DEPRECIATING it AT $1,850,000 ÷ 10 = <strong>$185,000 per year</strong> – RATHER than the $200,000 per YEAR it WOULD otherwise HAVE recognized.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3805 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Amount of investment tax credits recognized during the period</span><span>Accounting policy adopted for presentation (cost reduction, deferred credit, expense reduction, or separate income)</span><span>Unclaimed or unrecognized investment tax credits, and circumstances giving rise to non-recognition</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether reasonable assurance of entitlement genuinely exists, particularly for SR&ED claims subject to review</span><span>Whether expenditures genuinely qualify under the relevant tax legislation</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Amount of qualifying expenditures, where the final credit amount is subject to tax authority assessment</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>A Note on International Comparability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely Canada-specific mechanism, without a direct standalone counterpart</div>
<div class="text-supporting">UNLIKE MOST other ASPE Sections COVERED throughout THIS poster LIBRARY, Section 3805 DOES not HAVE a DIRECTLY comparable STANDALONE counterpart WITHIN IFRS or MOST other FRAMEWORKS this LIBRARY has COVERED – OTHER jurisdictions' TAX credit REGIMES (e.g. RESEARCH and DEVELOPMENT tax INCENTIVES elsewhere) ARE typically ADDRESSED through THEIR own frameworks' GENERAL government ASSISTANCE or INCOME tax STANDARDS, RATHER than a DEDICATED, standalone SECTION. Section 3805'S existence AS its OWN Section REFLECTS the SPECIFIC, WIDESPREAD significance of THE SR&amp;ED program TO Canadian PRIVATE enterprise PRACTICE.</div>
<div class="note">GIVEN this Section'S GENUINELY jurisdiction-SPECIFIC nature, a FORMAL side-by-SIDE comparison table (AS used ELSEWHERE in this LIBRARY) is LESS meaningful HERE than FOR most OTHER ASPE Sections.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ITC event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>ITC on current expenditures</span><span><span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Reduces the related expense, or presented as income</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>ITC on depreciable assets</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Reduces asset cost, or deferred and amortized</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3800</span><span>Government Assistance – supplies the parallel recognition philosophy and presentation choices this Section specializes for tax credits</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3465</span><span>Income Taxes – the tax accounting framework this Section's credits directly interact with</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Investment tax credit (ITC)</div><div class="glossary-def">A reduction in income taxes otherwise payable, earned by incurring specified qualifying expenditures under Canadian federal or provincial tax legislation.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">SR&ED (Scientific Research and Experimental Development)</div><div class="glossary-def">Canada's primary tax incentive program for qualifying research and development expenditures, the most common source of investment tax credits for private enterprises.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
